<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaspar Yanga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1545">1545</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Yanga, Ñanga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaspar Yanga was a Central African man who led a maroon colony of enslaved Africans in the highlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, under early Spanish colonial rule. Thought to be of Bantu descent, he was kidnapped and sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and around 1570 escaped a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantation with others to the highlands near Mt. Orizaba, where their palenque grew to about 500 people and raided caravans on the Camino Real. In 1609 the maroons held off a Spanish force of about 550, and the stalemate ended in a treaty granting them freedom and self-rule, achieved sometime between 1618 and 1641. Their town, San Lorenzo de los <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a>, was the first free town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and was renamed Yanga in 1932.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In New Spain in 1609 the maroons of <b>Gaspar Yanga</b> won recognition as a free town.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>Many resisted by running away, changing masters or forming maroon communities (palenques): in 1609 <b>Gaspar Yanga</b>’s maroons near Orizaba won recognition as a free town.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
